-- title:
--   Theorem 3.8, p. 25 — Algorithm 3.1's objective values are nonincreasing; for bounded 𝒳 the iterates are bounded and the values converge
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathcal P$ is the set of all probability distributions on $\mathbb R^k$ with mean $\mu$ and covariance matrix $\Sigma\succ0$, $\Omega=\begin{bmatrix}\Sigma+\mu\mu^\top&\mu\\\mu^\top&1\end{bmatrix}$ is the second-order moment matrix, $\langle X,Y\rangle=\mathrm{Tr}(XY)$, and $\epsilon\in(0,1)$.
--
--   Consider the joint chance constraint with $m\ge1$ rows, its worst-case CVaR functional $\mathcal J(x,\alpha)$ (31), a set $\mathcal X\subseteq\mathbb R^n$, a cost vector $c\in\mathbb R^n$, a tolerance $\delta>0$ and $\bar{\mathcal A}=\{\alpha:\alpha\ge\delta e\}$. Assume that $x_{\mathrm{init}}$ is feasible in problem (45) for some $\alpha\in\bar{\mathcal A}$, i.e. $x_{\mathrm{init}}\in\mathcal X$ and $\mathcal J(x_{\mathrm{init}},\alpha)\le0$. Let $(x^t)$, $(\alpha^t)$ be a run of Algorithm 3.1 from $x_{\mathrm{init}}$ and $f^t=c^\top x^t$. Then
--
--   1. the sequence $(f^t)_{t\ge0}$ is monotonically decreasing: $f^{t+1}\le f^t$ for all $t$;
--   2. if $\mathcal X$ is bounded, then $(x^t)$ is bounded and $(f^t)$ converges to a finite limit.
--
--   The alternating minimization over $x$ and $\alpha$ thus never worsens the objective, although it need not reach the global optimum of (44).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: {f^t} is monotonically decreasing → Antitone f (f^{t+1} ≤ f^t); reason: the proof shows that x^{t−1} stays feasible, which gives f^t ≤ f^{t−1}; strict decrease fails when the iteration stalls, e.g. when α^t = α^{t−1} is already optimal
--   - reading: the sequences generated by Algorithm 3.1, which stops at the test of Step 4 → any pair of sequences satisfying Steps 1–3 for all t (IsAlg31Run); reason: the termination test only truncates the run; the conclusions for the full run hold for every truncation
--   - generalization: 𝒳 ⊆ ℝ^n convex, closed and SDP-representable (p. 1) → an arbitrary set X ⊆ ℝ^n; reason: the conclusions use neither convexity nor closedness; the source's 𝒳 is a special case
--   - generalization: δ > 0 a small tolerance, set to 10⁻⁷ → any δ > 0; reason: the statement is uniform in δ; δ = 10⁻⁷ is a special case
--   - encoding: max_{i=1,…,m} with m ≥ 1 implicit → ⨆ over Fin m with the hypothesis 0 < m; reason: the max is the finite supremum for m ≥ 1; standing convention of the published 02 items
--   - encoding: bounded sequence; finite limit → Bornology.IsBounded (range x); ∃ L : ℝ, Tendsto f atTop (𝓝 L); reason: Lean convention; same meaning
-- source:
--   Zymler, Kuhn, Rustem, Distributionally Robust Joint Chance Constraints with Second-Order Moment Information, Math. Program. 137 (2013), accepted manuscript of 13 Aug 2011, p. 25, Theorem 3.8; p. 24, (45), (46), Algorithm 3.1; p. 23, Ā

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DRJointCC_Individual_Supplement_Algorithm

open MeasureTheory
open scoped ENNReal

namespace DRJointCC.Individual.Supplement

/-- Theorem 3.8, p. 25: if `x_init ∈ 𝒳` is feasible in (45) for some `α ∈ Ā`, then along every
run of Algorithm 3.1 the objective values `f^t = cᵀx^t` are monotonically decreasing
(nonincreasing); if `𝒳` is bounded, the iterates `x^t` are bounded and `f^t` converges to a
finite limit. -/
theorem theorem_3_8 {m n k : ℕ} (hm : 0 < m) (D : DRJointCC.Joint.JCCData m n k)
    (μ : Fin k → ℝ) (Sig : Matrix (Fin k) (Fin k) ℝ) (hSig : Sig.PosDef)
    (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) (hε1 : ε < 1) (δ : ℝ) (hδ : 0 < δ)
    (X : Set (Fin n → ℝ)) (c : Fin n → ℝ) (xinit : Fin n → ℝ)
    (hinit : xinit ∈ X ∧ ∃ α₀ ∈ Abar m δ, DRJointCC.Joint.Jfun D μ Sig ε xinit α₀ ≤ 0)
    (x : ℕ → Fin n → ℝ) (α : ℕ → Fin m → ℝ)
    (hrun : IsAlg31Run D μ Sig ε δ X c xinit x α) :
    Antitone (fun t => c ⬝ᵥ x t) ∧
      (Bornology.IsBounded X →
        Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range x) ∧
          ∃ L : ℝ, Filter.Tendsto (fun t => c ⬝ᵥ x t) Filter.atTop (nhds L)) := by sorry

end DRJointCC.Individual.Supplement
